 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. The shader loop runs for each sample along the ray, and for each sample, it samples all $N$ volumes.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the sum of the sizes of the 3D textures for all volumes, limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` and `MaxMemoryFraction`.
-- **Measured Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on GPU memory bandwidth and the number of volumes. Rendering multiple volumes increases the number of texture fetches per sample, potentially reducing frame rates compared to single-volume rendering.
-- **Scaling:** Scales with the number of available GPU cores, as each pixel is processed independently in the fragment shader. However, the number of volumes is limited by `MAX_NUMBER_OF_VOLUMES` (4 or 5 depending on platform) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simultaneous visualization of multiple datasets (e.g., anatomical structures and functional data) in a single pass. Efficient use of GPU shaders for parallel processing. Support for adaptive sampling and masking.
-- **Limitations:** Limited to a small number of volumes (up to 5). Requires careful management of GPU memory. Hardware loop limits may restrict the maximum number of samples per ray. Silent failures for invalid cropping planes can make debugging difficult [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When visualizing multiple co-registered volumetric datasets is required, and real-time interaction is needed.
-- **When Not to Use:** When the number of volumes exceeds hardware limits, or when memory constraints prevent loading all volumes into GPU memory.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M \cdot R)$, where $N$ is the number of volumes, $M$ is the number of samples per ray, and $R$ is the number of rays (pixels). Adaptive sampling can reduce $M$ and $R$ dynamically [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V)$, where $V$ is the volume of each dataset in GPU memory. Limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance depends heavily on GPU hardware, resolution, and scene complexity. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) helps maintain frame rates by reducing sample density when render time exceeds allocated time [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales with the number of GPUs and their memory capacity. `MaxMemoryFraction` controls how much of the allocated memory is used for textures [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes, hardware acceleration via shaders, adaptive sampling, masking support [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited by GPU memory and loop iteration limits, complex setup for multi-volume alignment, potential performance overhead from `ReportProgress` on small datasets [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When multiple volume datasets need to be visualized simultaneously with accurate compositing and shading [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When only a single volume is rendered (use [Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering via 3D Texturing](2799-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering-via-3d-texturing.md)), or when GPU memory is severely constrained [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension of the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to add support for multiple input datasets and properties [S1, unofficial]. The project utilizes GLSL shaders for general GPU programming, chosen for their speed and broader device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of direct volume ray casting and hardware-accelerated rendering [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension to the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian, building on the original `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` by Ken Martin, Will Schroeder, and Bill Lorensen. The project uses GLSL shaders for GPU programming, chosen for their speed and broader device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. It is used in scientific visualization and medical imaging applications where multiple volumetric datasets (e.g., different modalities or time steps) need to be rendered together [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
